To change the Currency field from an Autocomplete search to a standard Dropdown (Select) while maintaining perfect visual consistency and state management across all dynamic rows, we will replace the Autocomplete component with a TextField select.
This approach natively leverages your existing handleRowChange function and perfectly matches the styling of your "Txn Type" dropdown.
### The Code Replacement
Find the `` Grid block inside your rows.map loop (around line 590) and replace that entire <Grid item...> block with the following code:
```jsx
{/* Currency */}
<Grid item size={{ xl: 1.5, lg: 2 }}>
  <Tooltip title="Select Currency" placement="top-start">
    <TextField
      select
      label="Currency *"
      value={row.currency || ""}
      fullWidth
      InputLabelProps={{ shrink: true }}
      onChange={(e) => {
        handleRowChange(row.id, "currency", e.target.value);
        // Clear amount when currency changes, preserving your original logic
        handleRowChange(row.id, "amount", ""); 
      }}
      SelectProps={{
        displayEmpty: true,
        // Shows a gray placeholder when no currency is selected
        renderValue: (selected) => !selected ? <span style={{ color: 'gray', opacity: 0.8 }}>Currency</span> : selected,
        MenuProps: {
          PaperProps: {
            style: {
              maxHeight: 250, // Prevents the dropdown from getting too long if you have many currencies
            },
          },
        }
      }}
    >
      {currencyOptions.map((currency) => (
        <MenuItem key={currency} value={currency}>
          {currency}
        </MenuItem>
      ))}
    </TextField>
  </Tooltip>
</Grid>

```
### Why this works flawlessly for multiple rows:
 1. **State Isolation:** Because the value is tied explicitly to row.currency (from the .map iteration), and the onChange passes the unique row.id, each row manages its own currency state independently without bleeding into other rows.
 2. **Visual Consistency:** By using <TextField select fullWidth InputLabelProps={{ shrink: true }}>, it perfectly mirrors the height, border radius, and label behavior of the "Amount" and "Type" inputs next to it, maintaining your grid symmetry.
 3. **Placeholder Logic:** The renderValue prop ensures that when a new row is added (where currency is ""), it displays a clean, greyed-out "Currency" placeholder just like the "Txn Type" box does.
